  "answer": "To reduce the risk of image classifiers mispredicting when exposed to pixel-level perturbations or distribution shifts, researchers propose pre-training vision models on a large-scale external dataset before fine-tuning them for specific target tasks (http://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/hendrycks19a/hendrycks19a.pdf). Training models from scratch on limited target data yields feature representations that fail to generalize well to distribution shifts or perturbations (http://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/hendrycks19a/hendrycks19a.pdf). By instead adopting a \"pre-train then fine-tune\" paradigm on a diverse dataset (such as Downsampled ImageNet), the model learns transferable feature representations that significantly improve out-of-distribution (OOD) detection, noise tolerance, adversarial robustness, and calibration on downstream tasks (http://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/hendrycks19a/hendrycks19a.pdf). Following this pre-training, the entire network is briefly fine-tuned on the target dataset using a low learning rate and no dropout, a mechanism that limits the model's overfitting to noise and secures these robustness and uncertainty benefits (http://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/hendrycks19a/hendrycks19a.pdf)."
